    "narrative": "For our August 29th slate, **Seiya Suzuki** (75.03) and **Brice Turang** (71.96) anchor the lineup as our most efficient starts. **Seiya Suzuki** benefits from an elite matchup against Abbott, carrying a +19% SP skill advantage and a favorable park factor, while **Brice Turang** is a mandatory play; beyond his lead-off status, his matchup against Quantrill features a slow pitcher delivery that significantly boosts his stolen base equity\u2014a critical lever for us in this tight 5x5 race. **Corbin Carroll** (70.86) remains locked in the two-hole, providing the high-floor speed production we demand. While **Josh Naylor** (66.23) and **Gabriel Moreno** (62.94) both clear the 60-point threshold, they serve as steady secondary contributors against favorable matchups, though we are monitoring Wilkinson\u2019s high xERA of 6.01, which should allow **Gabriel Moreno** to maximize his RBI opportunities.\n\nThe final decision in the outfield was a tight contest between **Jose Caballero** (62.07) and **Mickey Moniak** (55.11). **Jose Caballero** secured the start over **Mickey Moniak** due to his robust +13% dynamic platoon edge against Gamboa, which outweighed his suboptimal ninth-place spot in the order. **Griffin Conine** (43.19) barely clung to his spot, as the optimizer heavily penalized him for the -17% SP skill differential against Cavalli, though he remains active for his home run upside. On the bench, the absence of **Vinnie Pasquantino** is a clear efficiency loss, but our injury report remains the primary bottleneck with **James Wood**, **Trevor Story**, and **Giancarlo Stanton** all confirmed unavailable due to IL stints. We will keep a close eye on the late-day reports to ensure no weather-related risks emerge for these matchups, particularly for **Max Muncy** (58.18), whose marginal score makes him a candidate to be pulled if his seventh-place lineup position shifts further down or if park conditions deteriorate.",
